{"chunk_id": "4864c13e48e6", "text": "This section explains the unusual births that shape the future conflict of the Kuru dynasty. Bhishma arranges the marriage of Vidura to a woman of mixed caste, and Vidura fathers worthy children. More importantly, the narration turns to the births of Dhritarashtra\u2019s hundred sons and Pandu\u2019s five sons. Gandhari, granted a boon by the sage Vyasa to bear a hundred sons, carries her pregnancy for two years. In frustration after hearing that Kunti has already borne a radiant son, she strikes her womb, producing only a lump of flesh. Vyasa divides it into 101 pieces and places them in pots of clarified butter, from which the Kauravas are eventually born, beginning with Duryodhana. His birth is marked by terrifying omens, and sages advise Dhritarashtra to abandon him for the good of the kingdom\u2014but the king refuses, setting the stage for the tragic future of the Kuru line.\nGandhari\u2019s boon leads to the miraculous birth of the Kauravas, with ominous signs at Duryodhana\u2019s birth and ignored advice to abandon him.\nThemes: miraculous birth, omens and fate, dynastic succession, parental attachment vs duty, divine intervention", "vector": [0.0068700555711984634, -0.013506061397492886, -0.012962238863110542, -0.009341347962617874, -0.005228263791650534, 0.013870904222130775, -0.022936899214982986, -0.006226418074220419, -0.03262932226061821, -0.04105512052774429, 0.0197841078042984, 0.014813987538218498, -0.000868652481585741, 0.022620243951678276, -0.008535941131412983, 0.02175288088619709, 0.037117574363946915, -0.00508026173338294, 0.032546717673540115, -0.030454033985733986, -0.017581284046173096, -0.011826408095657825, 0.007654811721295118, 0.025566520169377327, -0.03447418659925461, 0.03494228795170784, 0.020307278260588646, -0.021353619173169136, 0.00047498379717580974, -0.013712575659155846, -0.033978551626205444, 0.010518481954932213, 0.005001097451895475, -0.01868269592523575, -0.024175986647605896, 0.003834